WEBVTT

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:04.840
Critical thinking improves the quality of questions, evidence evaluation, assumptions,

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.000
reasoning, and decisions.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.000
It is not about rejecting every claim or trying to win every argument.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.215
It is about making conclusions more reliable by examining how they were formed and how

00:00:13.215 --> 00:00:13.995
strongly they are supported.

00:00:13.995 --> 00:00:16.000
The first habit is to identify the claim.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Separate facts, interpretations, opinions, predictions, and recommendations.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Ask what would need to be true, what evidence is relevant,

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.995
whether the evidence is sufficient, and what is missing.

00:00:24.995 --> 00:00:28.995
Strong conclusions require support that fits the importance and consequence of the claim.

00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:32.000
The second habit is to examine assumptions and framing.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Ask what is being taken for granted, how the problem has been defined,

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.000
what context matters, and whether incentives or stakeholders change the interpretation.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.930
Framing shapes which evidence is noticed and which solutions appear reasonable,

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:44.935
so it deserves deliberate attention.

00:00:44.935 --> 00:00:47.000
The third habit is to inspect reasoning.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.990
Look at premises and conclusions, cause and effect, correlation and causation,

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:54.720
necessary and sufficient conditions, false dilemmas, circular reasoning,

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:55.995
and overgeneralization.

00:00:55.995 --> 00:00:59.790
Then account for predictable thinking traps such as confirmation bias, anchoring,

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:03.929
availability, overconfidence, sunk cost, motivated reasoning, and groupthink.

00:01:03.929 --> 00:01:07.995
The final habit is to communicate uncertainty honestly and review decisions over time.

00:01:07.995 --> 00:01:12.095
Evaluate sources, use AI-generated material critically, make tradeoffs visible,

00:01:12.095 --> 00:01:15.000
document reasoning, and welcome constructive disagreement.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.090
The goal is practical judgment: better conclusions, better decisions,

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:21.860
fewer avoidable errors, and more reliable action under real conditions.
